1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:08,033
How much would you pay to play a game with an infinite
expected payout? Ten dollars? A hundred? Your life savings?

2
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:13,133
Before you answer, let me show you the game. The rules fit on
a single coin.

3
00:00:13,433 --> 00:00:18,100
We flip a fair coin until the first heads. Each toss is
independent.

4
00:00:18,267 --> 00:00:23,400
Heads on the first toss pays two dollars. Tails, then heads,
pays four.

5
00:00:23,567 --> 00:00:28,600
Two tails, then heads, pays eight. Every extra tails doubles
the prize.

6
00:00:28,767 --> 00:00:35,033
The entry fee is separate. This is the prize you receive
before subtracting what you paid to play.

7
00:00:35,333 --> 00:00:40,733
Now match each prize with its probability. Two dollars has a
one-in-two chance.

8
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:45,967
Four dollars has a one-in-four chance. Eight dollars has a
one-in-eight chance.

9
00:00:46,133 --> 00:00:54,700
Multiply each prize by its probability. Each possible stopping
point contributes exactly one dollar to the expected payout.

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:02,667
One dollar, plus one dollar, plus one dollar. With no limit on
the number of tosses, the sum never stops growing.

11
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:08,000
The expected payout is infinite. This is the Saint Petersburg
paradox.

12
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:16,400
But infinite expectation does not mean an infinite prize. The
game ends with a finite payout with probability one.

13
00:01:16,567 --> 00:01:22,800
Half the time, you get just two dollars. Three quarters of the
time, you get four dollars or less.

14
00:01:22,967 --> 00:01:27,067
And seven out of eight times, the prize is eight dollars or
less.

15
00:01:27,367 --> 00:01:33,267
So what is pulling the average upward? Extremely rare prizes
that are even more enormous.

16
00:01:33,433 --> 00:01:40,167
A first heads on toss twenty pays over a million dollars. Its
probability is about one in a million.

17
00:01:40,333 --> 00:01:46,367
That outcome still adds exactly one dollar to the expected
payout, just like the first toss.

18
00:01:46,667 --> 00:01:53,733
Your hesitation is not a failure of arithmetic. You have a
finite wallet, and you only get to play once.

19
00:01:53,900 --> 00:02:01,067
Losing money you need can matter far more than the same number
of dollars added to an already huge fortune.

20
00:02:01,233 --> 00:02:09,133
Expected utility models that difference. It values outcomes
through your wealth and preferences, rather than dollars
alone.

21
00:02:09,433 --> 00:02:17,033
And a real organizer cannot promise unlimited money. Put a
ceiling on the payout, and you have changed the game.

22
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:27,033
For example, cap every prize at one thousand twenty-four
dollars. Now the expected payout is just eleven dollars,
before the entry fee.

23
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:34,933
That ceiling removes the endless chain of larger and larger
prizes that made the original expectation infinite.

24
00:02:35,233 --> 00:02:44,367
There is no single ticket price everyone must accept. A
mathematical expectation and a personal decision answer
different questions.

25
00:02:44,533 --> 00:02:49,567
The average can be infinite. Your wallet, and your willingness
to risk it, are not.

26
00:02:49,767 --> 00:02:51,267
Smarter choices.

27
00:02:51,567 --> 00:02:53,000
A better future.

28
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:54,733
Follow for more.
